1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,847
So I'll be speaking to you using language because I can this is one these magical abilities that we humans have.
所以，我会用语言和大家说话，因为我能做到；这是人类拥有的奇妙能力之一。

2
00:00:08,174 --> 00:00:12,126
We can transmit really complicated thoughts to one another.
我们能够把非常复杂的思想传递给彼此。

3
00:00:12,539 --> 00:00:17,373
So what I'm doing right now is I'm making sounds with my mouth as I'm exhaling.
所以，我现在所做的，就是在呼气时用嘴发出声音。

4
00:00:17,397 --> 00:00:22,779
I'm making tones and hisses and puffs and those are creating air vibrations in the air.
我发出音调、嘶声和气流声，它们在空气中产生振动。

5
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:27,058
Those air vibrations are traveling to you they're hitting your eardrums.
这些空气振动传到你们这里，到达你们的耳膜。

6
00:00:27,082 --> 00:00:38,450
And then your brain takes those vibrations from your eardrums and transforms them into thoughts I hope laughter I hope that's happening.
然后大脑接收来自耳膜的振动，把它们变成思想——希望如此。（观众笑）我希望这正在发生。

7
00:00:38,474 --> 00:00:45,766
So because of this ability we humans are able to transmit our ideas across vast reaches of space and time.
因此，凭借这种能力，人类能够跨越广阔的空间与漫长的时间传递思想。

8
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:53,511
We're able to transmit knowledge across minds I can put a bizarre new idea in your mind right now.
我们能够在人与人之间传递知识；我现在就能把一个古怪的新想法放进你们脑中。

9
00:00:53,535 --> 00:01:01,459
I could say imagine a jellyfish waltzing in a library while thinking about quantum mechanics laughter.
我可以说：想象一只水母在图书馆里跳华尔兹，同时思考量子力学。（观众笑）

10
00:01:02,281 --> 00:01:08,069
Now if everything has gone relatively well in your life so far you probably haven't had that thought before laughter.
如果你们迄今为止的生活还算顺利，你们大概从没想过这个画面。（观众笑）

11
00:01:08,495 --> 00:01:11,406
But now I've just made you think it through language.
但现在，我刚刚通过语言让你们想到了它。

12
00:01:11,714 --> 00:01:17,107
Now of course there isn't just one language in the world there are about 7 000 languages spoken around the world.
当然，世界上不只有一种语言；她说，全世界使用着大约七千种语言。

13
00:01:17,131 --> 00:01:20,291
And all the languages differ from one another in all kinds of ways.
而各种语言在方方面面都彼此不同。

14
00:01:20,315 --> 00:01:29,123
Some languages have different sounds they have different vocabularies and they also have different structures very importantly different structures.
有些语言有不同的声音、不同的词汇，也有不同的结构；结构上的差异非常重要。

15
00:01:29,956 --> 00:01:33,784
That begs the question does the language we speak shape the way we think.
这就引出了一个问题：我们说的语言，会塑造我们的思考方式吗？

16
00:01:33,808 --> 00:01:38,575
Now this is an ancient question people have been speculating about this question forever.
这是个古老的问题，人们长久以来一直在对此进行推测。

17
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:46,503
Charlemagne holy roman emperor said to have a second language is to have a second soul strong statement that language crafts reality.
她引用神圣罗马帝国皇帝查理曼的话：“拥有第二种语言，就拥有第二个灵魂。”这是语言塑造现实的有力表态。

18
00:01:47,052 --> 00:01:53,575
But on the other hand shakespeare has juliet say what's in a name a rose by any other name would smell as sweet.
但另一方面，莎士比亚让朱丽叶说：名字有什么意义？玫瑰换个名字，也同样芬芳。

19
00:01:54,564 --> 00:01:57,617
Well that suggests that maybe language doesn't craft reality.
这又暗示，也许语言并不会塑造现实。

20
00:01:57,986 --> 00:02:01,992
These arguments have gone back and forth for thousands of years.
这些论点来来回回争论了几千年。

21
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:06,771
But until recently there hasn't been any data to help us decide either way.
但她说，直到不久前，都没有数据帮助人们判断哪一种说法更合理。

22
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:15,619
Recently in my lab and other labs around the world we've started doing research and now we have actual scientific data to weigh in on this question.
最近，我的实验室和世界各地其他实验室开始研究这个问题，现在有了真正的科学数据参与讨论。

23
00:02:15,978 --> 00:02:18,519
So let me tell you about some of my favorite examples.
所以，让我给大家讲几个我最喜欢的例子。

24
00:02:18,906 --> 00:02:24,072
I'll start with an example from an aboriginal community in australia that I had the chance to work with.
我先从澳大利亚的一个原住民社区讲起，我曾有机会与这个社区合作。

25
00:02:24,096 --> 00:02:29,657
These are the kuuk thaayorre people they live in pormpuraaw at the very west edge of cape York.
这是 Kuuk Thaayorre 人，他们住在约克角最西端的 Pormpuraaw。

26
00:02:30,411 --> 00:02:35,729
What's cool about kuuk thaayorre is in kuuk thaayorre they don't use words like left and right.
她说，Kuuk Thaayorre 语有趣的地方在于，他们不用类似“左”和“右”的词。

27
00:02:35,753 --> 00:02:39,885
And instead everything is in cardinal directions north south east and west.
而是用基本方位来表达一切：北、南、东、西。

28
00:02:40,485 --> 00:02:43,020
And when I say everything I really mean everything.
而且我说“一切”，真的是指一切。

29
00:02:43,044 --> 00:02:50,894
You would say something like oh there's an ant on your southwest leg or move your cup to the north northeast a little bit.
你可能会这样说：哦，你西南边那条腿上有只蚂蚁；或者，把杯子往北偏东北挪一点。

30
00:02:51,464 --> 00:02:56,234
In fact the way that you say hello in kuuk thaayorre is you say which way are you going.
她说，事实上，用 Kuuk Thaayorre 语打招呼时，你会问：“你往哪个方向去？”

31
00:02:56,258 --> 00:03:01,191
And the answer should be north northeast in the far distance how about you.
回答则应该是：“往远处的北偏东北方向。你呢？”

32
00:03:01,215 --> 00:03:08,639
So imagine as you're walking around your day every person you greet you have to report your heading direction laughter.
想象一下，一天里走来走去，每碰到一个打招呼的人，你都得报告自己的行进方向。（观众笑）

33
00:03:09,242 --> 00:03:12,573
But that would actually get you oriented pretty fast right.
不过，这样其实很快就能让你弄清方向，对吧？

34
00:03:12,597 --> 00:03:17,656
Because you literally couldn't get past hello if you didn't know which way you were going.
因为如果不知道自己往哪里走，你连打招呼这一关都过不了。

35
00:03:19,029 --> 00:03:22,521
In fact people who speak languages like this stay oriented really well.
她说，事实上，说这类语言的人对方向掌握得非常好。

36
00:03:22,545 --> 00:03:25,470
They stay oriented better than we used to think humans could.
他们辨别方向的能力，比我们过去认为人类能达到的水平还要好。

37
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:30,492
We used to think that humans were worse than other creatures because of some biological excuse.
我们过去会拿某种生物学上的理由，认为人类比其他生物差。

38
00:03:30,516 --> 00:03:33,841
Oh we don't have magnets in our beaks or in our scales.
比如说：哦，我们的喙或鳞片里没有磁体。

39
00:03:33,865 --> 00:03:38,080
No if your language and your culture trains you to do it actually you can do it.
不，她认为，如果语言和文化训练你这样做，实际上你就能做到。

40
00:03:38,104 --> 00:03:41,144
There are humans around the world who stay oriented really well.
世界各地确实有人能非常好地保持方位感。
